--  Signals as events (D449, D477): the concept a provider that takes
--  turns supplies when it delivers the system's signals to a fibre that
--  waits for them, the portable names of the signals a server cares
--  about, and what one arrival is.  A signal never runs a handler of
--  Landin code: the provider reads it, through signalfd on Linux and an
--  EVFILT_SIGNAL filter of kqueue on Darwin and FreeBSD, as it reads any
--  other event, and wakes the fibre that waits.  It is a module beneath
--  core/io, as core/io/fake is, because the concept refines
--  `fibre.turns` and core/fibre already reaches core/io through
--  core/failure (D482).

import core/fibre
import core/io

--- The terminal's interrupt, SIGINT: someone pressed Ctrl-C.
public interrupt: atom
--- A request to end, SIGTERM: what a service manager sends to stop a
--- server, and what `kill` sends when it is not told otherwise.
public terminate: atom
--- The controlling terminal has gone, SIGHUP; a daemon, which has none,
--- commonly takes it to read its configuration again.
public hangup: atom
--- A child process ended, stopped or went on, SIGCHLD.
public child: atom
--- The first signal left to programs, SIGUSR1.
public user1: atom
--- The second signal left to programs, SIGUSR2.
public user2: atom

--- The signals a provider of `signals` delivers, by portable names: each
--- system numbers them its own way, and a program that names one here
--- means the same signal on every system.
public kind: type = interrupt | terminate | hangup | child | user1 | user2

--- One arrival of a watched signal: its portable name and the number the
--- system gave it, as a log or `kill` would show it.
public event: type = struct
    which: kind
    number: u32
end event

--- A provider that delivers signals to a fibre as events (D449). Only a
--- provider of `fibre.turns` supplies it (D477): a fibre waiting for a
--- signal lets the provider's other fibres run, where over a provider that
--- runs each task to its end it would hold the program before it served
--- anything. A program that waits for a signal asks for `signals`, and
--- over the blocking host it cannot be written, so a signal there keeps
--- the system's default disposition: SIGTERM ends the process.
---
--- `watch` takes a signal from the system's default disposition and
--- delivers it as an event from then on. `next_signal` waits as a receive
--- does, taking turns, for the next arrival of a watched signal; each
--- arrival is read once, by one waiter, and two arrivals of one signal
--- before it is read may be one event, as the systems' own queues keep
--- them. `stop_watching` gives every watched signal back to its default
--- disposition and wakes every fibre waiting in `next_signal`, whose call
--- fails `io_failed`, which is how a program stops the fibre that waits
--- (D449). `next_signal` is the only entry that may switch.
public signals: type = concept (provider: type) is fibre.turns
    --- Deliver `which` as an event from now on.
    watch: (self: provider, which: kind) -> none ! io.io_failed
    --- Wait, taking turns, for the next arrival of a watched signal.
    next_signal: (self: provider) -> (received: event) ! io.io_failed
    --- Give every watched signal back to its default disposition, and
    --- wake every waiter, failing its call.
    stop_watching: (self: provider) -> none ! io.io_failed
end signals

--- Deliver `which` as an event through `signals` evidence.
public watch: (provider: type is signals, host: provider, which: kind)
              -> none ! io.io_failed =
    try provider.watch(host, which)
end watch

--- Wait, taking turns, for the next arrival of a watched signal.
public next_signal: (provider: type is signals, host: provider)
                    -> (received: event) ! io.io_failed =
    received = try provider.next_signal(host)
end next_signal

--- Stop delivering signals as events, and wake every fibre waiting for
--- one, whose call fails `io_failed`.
public stop_watching: (provider: type is signals, host: provider)
                      -> none ! io.io_failed =
    try provider.stop_watching(host)
end stop_watching
